<section class="tool-guide" aria-labelledby="word-to-wordpress-guide">
<h2 id="word-to-wordpress-guide">How to move a Word draft into WordPress</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Choose your document.</strong> Use the button above or the file control in Open your Word document. Select a plain .docx up to 8 MiB. The file is read locally; nothing is uploaded. Cancelling the picker keeps any existing writing and pending preview.</li><li><strong>Inspect the conversion.</strong> Check the reading preview, exact article text, page details, and every notice. Only the main article is converted. If an unmarked table starts with column names, explicitly choose the first-row header option and preview the selected file again.</li><li><strong>Import after review.</strong> Acknowledge the conversion before choosing Import into editor. Replacing existing writing asks for confirmation and checkpoints the previous valid workspace. Source notes are kept; article fields and review questions are replaced and export approval is cleared.</li><li><strong>Prepare the post.</strong> Edit the article beside its reading preview. Compare facts with your source, inspect the WordPress handoff findings, and check the export review box. Copy or download WordPress blocks; the title stays separate. A complete WordPress kit is also available.</li><li><strong>Finish in WordPress.</strong> Create a new, empty draft post. Enter the title in its title field, open the post Options menu, and select Code editor. Paste the block source into the post body. Return to Visual editor, inspect the blocks, replace image placeholders, add post settings, and preview on desktop and mobile before publishing yourself.</li></ol>
<p>Use the full post Code editor. Themes, plugins, and editor versions can affect the result. This is a manual handoff with a limited article format, not a complete Word layout conversion. <a href="/help/publishing-handoff/#wordpress" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Detailed WordPress steps (new tab)</a>.</p>
<h2 id="word-import-limits">What stays and what changes?</h2>
<div class="article-table" role="region" aria-label="Word import support, scroll horizontally if needed" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">In your Word document</th><th scope="col">In the article and WordPress handoff</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Paragraphs and common heading styles</td><td>Editable text and headings. An opening title heading moves to the separate title field. WordPress body H1 headings become H2.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bold, italic, and safe web links</td><td>Common emphasis and absolute HTTP(S) hyperlinks. Unsupported styling may be simplified with notices; link availability is not checked.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ordinary bullets and decimal lists</td><td>Editable lists with supported starts, continuation, and nesting. Up to six levels and 200 items per continuous group. Custom or lettered numbering needs simplification first.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Simple rectangular tables</td><td>Editable tables with a column-header row. Unmarked headers require an explicit choice. Up to twelve columns and 100 data rows. Merged or nested tables are rejected.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Images and embedded objects</td><td>Visible text placeholders. No image or embedded-file bytes are extracted or loaded. Add permitted images in WordPress and review their alternative text.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Headers, footers, comments, author metadata, fonts, colors, and page layout</td><td>Excluded or simplified. Keep the original Word document and compare it with the preview.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tracked changes, fields, footnotes, endnotes, equations, and complex structures</td><td>Rejected before replacing writing. Resolve changes, automatic tables of contents, and unsupported structures in Word, or import a smaller formatted selection.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The converted article is limited to 60,000 characters. Old .doc files, macro-enabled or encrypted documents, damaged archives, and unusually packaged files are not supported. If compressed Word import is unavailable in your browser, copy a formatted selection instead. A rejected file can be discarded with Clear selected file or import preview.</p>
<h2 id="word-import-questions">Common questions</h2>
<details><summary>Is this Word to WordPress converter free?</summary><p>Yes. The public workflow needs no account, payment, WordPress connection, API credits, or private AI pilot access. Opening a file, converting, reviewing, and preparing exports run in this tab.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does my document leave this page?</summary><p>No document upload or server conversion happens here. Selected bytes and pending previews stay temporarily in the tab and are cleared on apply or clearing the import. Article text remains after apply. There is no autosave, browser persistence, or account history. Save an explicit private backup before leaving. Links you choose to open are handled by their destination.</p></details>
<details><summary>Will the Word layout look identical in WordPress?</summary><p>No. This tool prepares editable article structure. It omits or simplifies document-only content, styling, and page layout. Inspect the conversion and its notices, keep the original .docx, and check the actual WordPress post before publishing. It does not verify facts, grammar, source quality, or image permissions.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can I use a Google Docs draft?</summary><p>You can select an existing .docx exported by your document editor, or open Paste writing, keep source notes, or use more tools and choose Paste formatted writing. Inspect the conversion before applying it. This site does not connect to Google Drive.</p></details>
<details><summary>What if I already have Markdown?</summary><p>Use the same editor here or the <a href="/markdown-to-wordpress/">Markdown to WordPress converter</a>. Pasting into the article body keeps plain text or Markdown. The separate formatted import handles rich selections. Save existing work before navigating to another tool.</p></details>
